WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.275
Lesson 2.5.

00:00:02.275 --> 00:00:04.640
MEV and protecting your trades.

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.647
By the end, you'll set slippage and

00:00:06.647 --> 00:00:09.045
routing so your trades aren't easy targets.

00:00:10.345 --> 00:00:11.635
Here's the plan.

00:00:11.635 --> 00:00:13.888
What M.E.V. actually is, and which

00:00:13.888 --> 00:00:16.120
kind of it actually hurts you.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:19.195
The sandwich attack, mechanically, step by step.

00:00:19.195 --> 00:00:21.730
The real defences that work, and why.

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:24.975
And a full worked example, with real numbers, showing

00:00:24.975 --> 00:00:28.220
exactly what your own slippage setting exposes you to.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:31.165
What MEV actually is.

00:00:32.465 --> 00:00:34.479
Here's the precise definition, worth

00:00:34.479 --> 00:00:36.685
getting right before anything else.

00:00:36.685 --> 00:00:41.285
M.E.V., maximal extractable value, is profit taken by whoever

00:00:41.285 --> 00:00:45.665
decides the order transactions land in, inside a block.

00:00:45.665 --> 00:00:49.733
Some M.E.V. is genuinely harmless, arbitrage that simply

00:00:49.733 --> 00:00:53.087
realigns a pool's price with the wider market,

00:00:53.087 --> 00:00:56.670
the exact mechanism from Lesson two point two.

00:00:56.670 --> 00:00:59.042
The kind that actually hurts you is different,

00:00:59.042 --> 00:01:01.615
and it specifically targets your own pending trade.

00:01:02.915 --> 00:01:04.610
One real, dated number, so

00:01:04.610 --> 00:01:06.730
this isn't an abstract worry.

00:01:06.730 --> 00:01:10.521
Blockchain researchers have tracked hundreds of millions of

00:01:10.521 --> 00:01:14.312
dollars extracted from traders through sandwich attacks and

00:01:14.312 --> 00:01:18.150
similar MEV, across multiple years of on-chain activity.

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.136
This is outside research, not this program's own

00:01:21.136 --> 00:01:23.757
data, but it's exactly why the defences in

00:01:23.757 --> 00:01:26.499
this lesson are worth setting up before your

00:01:26.499 --> 00:01:28.815
next large trade, not after a bad one.

00:01:30.115 --> 00:01:32.313
Put the two kinds of M.E.V. side

00:01:32.313 --> 00:01:34.778
by side, since telling them apart is

00:01:34.778 --> 00:01:37.210
the whole point of this chapter.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.541
Arbitrage M.E.V. reacts to a price that's already

00:01:40.541 --> 00:01:43.072
out of line, and its actual effect is

00:01:43.072 --> 00:01:46.170
to correct it, closer to the wider market.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:49.269
Sandwich M.E.V. creates the bad price itself,

00:01:49.269 --> 00:01:52.166
purely to profit from your specific trade,

00:01:52.166 --> 00:01:55.130
and leaves nothing corrected once it's done.

00:01:56.430 --> 00:01:57.520
Quick check.

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.995
Is all MEV harmful to you as a trader?

00:02:04.415 --> 00:02:07.422
The answer: no. Arbitrage that realigns a

00:02:07.422 --> 00:02:10.143
pool's price with the wider market is

00:02:10.143 --> 00:02:12.090
a harmless form of MEV.

00:02:12.090 --> 00:02:14.261
This lesson is about the specific kind

00:02:14.261 --> 00:02:16.265
that targets your own pending trade.

00:02:17.565 --> 00:02:18.780
The sandwich attack.

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:23.995
Here's exactly how a sandwich attack unfolds, in order.

00:02:23.995 --> 00:02:26.204
A bot watches the public mempool, and

00:02:26.204 --> 00:02:28.946
sees your pending swap before it confirms.

00:02:28.946 --> 00:02:31.002
It buys the same token, just ahead

00:02:31.002 --> 00:02:33.006
of you, pushing the price up.

00:02:33.006 --> 00:02:36.235
Your trade then executes, at that now-worse price.

00:02:36.235 --> 00:02:38.705
And the bot immediately sells, just after

00:02:38.705 --> 00:02:41.005
you, capturing the gap it created.

00:02:41.005 --> 00:02:42.640
Your trade is the bread.

00:02:42.640 --> 00:02:44.335
The bot's two trades are the filling.

00:02:45.635 --> 00:02:48.112
Here's why your own slippage tolerance is

00:02:48.112 --> 00:02:50.352
exactly what caps how much a sandwich

00:02:50.352 --> 00:02:53.070
can actually take from you, mechanically.

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:56.275
Your slippage setting defines the single worst price

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:59.720
you'll accept before the transaction simply reverts.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:01.565
A sandwich bot can only push the

00:03:01.565 --> 00:03:03.635
price as far as that ceiling allows,

00:03:03.635 --> 00:03:06.095
since pushing further would just cause your

00:03:06.095 --> 00:03:08.800
trade to fail entirely, earning it nothing.

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:11.585
Your own setting is, literally, its profit ceiling.

00:03:12.885 --> 00:03:15.450
Here's how to actually check, after the fact,

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:18.260
whether a past trade of yours was sandwiched.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:21.155
Pull up your transaction on a block explorer.

00:03:21.155 --> 00:03:23.879
Look at the two transactions immediately before

00:03:23.879 --> 00:03:26.165
and after yours, in the same block.

00:03:26.165 --> 00:03:29.360
If the same address bought the identical token right

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:32.253
before you, and sold it right after, that's the

00:03:32.253 --> 00:03:34.845
signature of a sandwich, not a coincidence.

00:03:36.145 --> 00:03:37.235
Quick check.

00:03:37.235 --> 00:03:39.669
What specifically limits how much a sandwich

00:03:39.669 --> 00:03:41.400
bot can extract from your trade?

00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:47.414
The answer: your own slippage

00:03:47.414 --> 00:03:49.415
tolerance, and the pool's depth.

00:03:49.415 --> 00:03:51.662
The bot can't push the price further than

00:03:51.662 --> 00:03:54.658
your setting allows, or your transaction simply reverts.

00:03:55.958 --> 00:03:57.173
The real defences.

00:03:58.473 --> 00:04:01.078
Here are the four defences that actually work,

00:04:01.078 --> 00:04:03.318
in the order most worth setting up.

00:04:03.318 --> 00:04:07.342
Tight but realistic slippage, something like zero point one

00:04:07.342 --> 00:04:10.762
to zero point five percent for liquid major pairs,

00:04:10.762 --> 00:04:14.348
looser only when you specifically understand why.

00:04:14.348 --> 00:04:18.385
M.E.V.-protected or private transaction routing, offered

00:04:18.385 --> 00:04:21.588
by many wallets and aggregators directly.

00:04:21.588 --> 00:04:23.541
Split large trades, so no single

00:04:23.541 --> 00:04:25.558
trade is a big enough target.

00:04:25.558 --> 00:04:29.393
And avoid broadcasting huge swaps into thin, illiquid pairs.

00:04:30.693 --> 00:04:33.705
Zoom into the private-routing defence specifically, since

00:04:33.705 --> 00:04:36.966
it works completely differently from tightening slippage.

00:04:36.966 --> 00:04:40.542
A normal transaction sits visibly in the public mempool,

00:04:40.542 --> 00:04:43.602
where any bot can see it before it confirms.

00:04:43.602 --> 00:04:47.280
A private or protected route instead sends your transaction

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.012
directly, never appearing in that public mempool at all.

00:04:51.012 --> 00:04:53.473
With nothing to see, there's simply nothing

00:04:53.473 --> 00:04:55.207
for a sandwich bot to react to.

00:04:56.507 --> 00:04:59.938
Here's what turning on protected routing actually looks like,

00:04:59.938 --> 00:05:02.839
in practice, since it's simpler than it sounds.

00:05:02.839 --> 00:05:06.997
Check whether your wallet or aggregator offers a “private,”

00:05:06.997 --> 00:05:12.286
“protected,” or “M.E.V.-secure” routing option directly in its settings.

00:05:12.286 --> 00:05:14.574
Switch it on, ideally as your default

00:05:14.574 --> 00:05:16.801
for anything above a small size.

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:19.798
And confirm the transaction still lands normally;

00:05:19.798 --> 00:05:22.554
a private route changes where it's broadcast,

00:05:22.554 --> 00:05:24.652
not whether it eventually confirms.

00:05:25.952 --> 00:05:28.343
Worth being precise about the one mistake

00:05:28.343 --> 00:05:30.735
this lesson keeps circling back to, since

00:05:30.735 --> 00:05:33.312
it's the opposite failure to a sandwich.

00:05:33.312 --> 00:05:35.672
Slippage set too loose leaves room for

00:05:35.672 --> 00:05:37.847
a sandwich to profit inside it.

00:05:37.847 --> 00:05:41.028
Slippage set too tight, near zero, on a genuinely

00:05:41.028 --> 00:05:44.909
volatile pair, just makes ordinary price movement cause your

00:05:44.909 --> 00:05:48.789
transaction to fail outright, and a failed transaction still

00:05:48.789 --> 00:05:51.652
costs you the gas, from Lesson one point two.

00:05:52.952 --> 00:05:53.957
Worked example.

00:05:55.257 --> 00:05:58.404
Here's the exposure, at a slippage tolerance

00:05:58.404 --> 00:06:01.222
of three percent, on a $20,000 swap.

00:06:01.222 --> 00:06:03.877
Up to roughly $600 is the most a

00:06:03.877 --> 00:06:07.819
sandwich could take, before its own costs, since

00:06:07.819 --> 00:06:10.232
3% of $20,000 is exactly $600.

00:06:11.532 --> 00:06:13.634
Now tighten that same swap to zero

00:06:13.634 --> 00:06:16.095
point five percent slippage, and see how

00:06:16.095 --> 00:06:18.677
directly the exposure shrinks with it.

00:06:18.677 --> 00:06:20.612
At three percent, up to roughly

00:06:20.612 --> 00:06:22.787
six hundred dollars is exposed.

00:06:22.787 --> 00:06:26.052
At zero point five percent, that drops to roughly

00:06:26.052 --> 00:06:30.165
one hundred dollars, a six-fold reduction, from tightening one

00:06:30.165 --> 00:06:32.777
single setting, on the exact same trade.

00:06:34.077 --> 00:06:37.295
Plot exposure against slippage tolerance across a

00:06:37.295 --> 00:06:40.191
wider range, and the relationship is exactly

00:06:40.191 --> 00:06:41.907
as direct as it looks.

00:06:41.907 --> 00:06:45.872
Three percent slippage exposes roughly six hundred dollars.

00:06:45.872 --> 00:06:48.697
One percent exposes roughly two hundred.

00:06:48.697 --> 00:06:51.317
Zero point five percent, one hundred.

00:06:51.317 --> 00:06:53.887
And zero point one percent, roughly twenty

00:06:53.887 --> 00:06:56.876
dollars, though at that tightness, ordinary price

00:06:56.876 --> 00:06:59.746
movement starts making the trade fail outright,

00:06:59.746 --> 00:07:02.137
exactly the trade-off from a moment ago.

00:07:03.437 --> 00:07:06.287
And here's the third option, which isn't a tighter

00:07:06.287 --> 00:07:09.382
number at all, but a different mechanism entirely.

00:07:09.382 --> 00:07:13.053
Route that same twenty-thousand-dollar swap through a private

00:07:13.053 --> 00:07:16.250
or protected transaction path, and it's never visible

00:07:16.250 --> 00:07:18.978
in the public mempool in the first place.

00:07:18.978 --> 00:07:20.723
Not a smaller exposure.

00:07:20.723 --> 00:07:22.927
Effectively no exposure to a sandwich,

00:07:22.927 --> 00:07:24.283
from that route, at all.

00:07:25.584 --> 00:07:26.674
Quick check.

00:07:26.674 --> 00:07:29.634
Why does setting slippage tolerance near zero

00:07:29.634 --> 00:07:32.659
on a genuinely volatile pair actually backfire?

00:07:37.079 --> 00:07:40.981
The answer: ordinary price movement makes the transaction revert,

00:07:40.981 --> 00:07:43.878
and a failed transaction still costs you gas, so

00:07:43.878 --> 00:07:46.539
you've paid without the trade even happening.

00:07:47.839 --> 00:07:50.892
One last reason splitting large trades specifically

00:07:50.892 --> 00:07:53.599
works, worth naming before the checklist.

00:07:53.599 --> 00:07:56.434
A sandwich bot pays its own gas, twice, to

00:07:56.434 --> 00:07:59.138
set up and close its attack, so it needs

00:07:59.138 --> 00:08:02.339
enough expected profit to justify that cost.

00:08:02.339 --> 00:08:04.405
A large trade, sandwiched once,

00:08:04.405 --> 00:08:06.384
can easily clear that bar.

00:08:06.384 --> 00:08:09.879
That same total value, split into several smaller trades,

00:08:09.879 --> 00:08:13.254
may simply not be worth attacking, individually, at all.

00:08:14.554 --> 00:08:16.014
Here's your checklist.

00:08:16.014 --> 00:08:18.749
Do it now, on every trade of real size.

00:08:18.749 --> 00:08:21.335
Slippage is set specifically for this pair

00:08:21.335 --> 00:08:23.620
and this size, never left on whatever

00:08:23.620 --> 00:08:25.964
high default the interface picked.

00:08:25.964 --> 00:08:27.922
Protected or private routing is

00:08:27.922 --> 00:08:29.997
switched on for larger swaps.

00:08:29.997 --> 00:08:33.751
And large orders are split, or routed through genuinely

00:08:33.751 --> 00:08:37.572
deep liquidity, not broadcast as one big, visible target.

00:08:38.872 --> 00:08:40.117
Let's recap.

00:08:40.117 --> 00:08:43.899
M.E.V. is profit from ordering transactions in a block;

00:08:43.899 --> 00:08:48.522
arbitrage is harmless, sandwiching your own pending trade isn't.

00:08:48.522 --> 00:08:50.592
A sandwich bot's profit is capped

00:08:50.592 --> 00:08:53.632
by your own slippage tolerance, mechanically.

00:08:53.632 --> 00:08:57.530
Tight, realistic slippage, protected routing, splitting large

00:08:57.530 --> 00:08:59.982
trades and avoiding thin pools are the

00:08:59.982 --> 00:09:02.357
four defences that actually work.

00:09:02.357 --> 00:09:04.754
And private routing removes your exposure

00:09:04.754 --> 00:09:07.037
entirely, rather than just shrinking it.

00:09:08.337 --> 00:09:10.027
One last honest note, before

00:09:10.027 --> 00:09:11.492
you go set these up.

00:09:11.492 --> 00:09:14.337
None of these four defences make you invisible

00:09:14.337 --> 00:09:17.181
or untouchable; they raise the cost and shrink

00:09:17.181 --> 00:09:19.784
the payoff of attacking you, which is what

00:09:19.784 --> 00:09:22.747
actually changes a bot's decision to bother.

00:09:22.747 --> 00:09:27.532
Layered together, tight slippage, protected routing, splitting, deep pools,

00:09:27.532 --> 00:09:31.372
they cover almost every realistic case this lesson describes.

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:35.674
Set slippage for this specific pair and size, and

00:09:35.674 --> 00:09:39.337
switch on protected routing before your next large swap.

00:09:39.337 --> 00:09:43.168
Next up, Lesson 2.6: Advanced execution,

00:09:43.168 --> 00:09:46.532
limit, TWAP and intent-based orders.
